    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d621">

                <pb facs="0579" n="567" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg567"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867N.01/2282</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stettinius</hi>)<anchor n="26" xml:id="d621-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-02-19">February 19, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>At yesterday’s Cabinet meeting the Palestine resolution was thoroughly discussed.
                    The President expressed the opinion to Secretary Stimson that it was important
                    for his letter on the matter to be made public.<anchor n="27" xml:id="d621-f2"/>
                    The Secretary replied that he first wished to try the executive session method
                    and hoped that Mr. McCloy<anchor n="28" xml:id="d621-f3"/> and General
                        Handy<anchor n="29" xml:id="d621-f4"/> would be able to stop the resolution
                    in executive session. He <pb facs="0580" n="568" xml:id="pg568"/> added that
                    General Marshall was anxious that the letter not be made public but that he
                    would review the matter with the General again. I stated that the State
                    Department felt it was important for the letter to be made public.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>E[<hi rendition="#small-caps">dward</hi>] S[<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >tettinius</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d621-f1" n="26" type="source" xml:id="d621-f1-content">
                <p>Addressed to the Assistant Secretary of State (Long), the Director of the Office
                    of Near Eastern and African Affairs (Murray), and the Deputy Director
                    (Alling).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d621-f2" n="27" xml:id="d621-f2-content">
                <p>The letter (February 7, <ref target="#pg563">p. 563</ref>) was not made
                    public.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d621-f3" n="28" xml:id="d621-f3-content">
                <p>John J. McCloy, Assistant Secretary of War.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d621-f4" n="29" xml:id="d621-f4-content">
                <p>Maj. Gen. Thomas T. Handy of the War Department General Staff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
